  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Saturday)\nIssued at 1237 PM MDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nLatest water vapor imagery shows broad upper high pressure located \nacross the southern tier of the country with a digging 500hpa low \noff of the Pacific NW coast. Quick moving zonal flow is found \nacross the upper midwest. At the surface high pressure is located \nacross much of the western Dakotas with a weak surface trough \nsliding southeast across eastern SD. At 18Z skies were mainly \nclear to partly cloudy except for along the ND/SD border where \nmore clouds were found. Temperatures were in the mid 50s to mid \n60s. \n\nModels are still advertising 700hpa theta-e ridge and associated \nforcing to slide across southern tier counties overnight. Did trim\nback inherited pops but am still a bit concerned that we might be\noverdoing the forecast. Ensembles are only showing 10-20 percent \nprobabilities of seeing greater than 0.01 inch of rain across our \nsouthern counties, and less than that of seeing 0.05 inch of \nrainfall. \n\nSunday will looks to be dry to begin with and becoming breezy with \neasterly upslope flow. This will result in a few more clouds which\nwill aid in keeping temperatures down a bit more. Sunday night \ninto Monday morning main upper trough finally kicks out across \nregion with associated 700hpa theta-e ridge and forcing. Better \nmoisture tracks along with this feature and this is when our best \nchance of seeing rainfall occurs. As the moisture shield tracks \nwest to east it will become more widespread. Expecting more to \nfall is rain, although can't rule out some scattered \nthundershowers here or there through the period.\n\nThe remainder of the week looks to be seasonable temperature wise,\nwith rainfall chances returning back to the forecast area around\nmid-week.",
